==== Flash using web interface ====

<WRAP dangerous>
Flashing OpenWrt through the OEM web interface is not always possible: the OpenWrt factory image needs to have the correct magic that matches the OEM firmware version. So, depending on the OEM firmware version, different versions of OpenWrt can be installed through the OEM web interface:

^ OEM firmware  ^ OpenWrt version accepted by OEM web interface                    ^
| 1.0.X         | 18.06.X, 19.07.0                                                 |
| 1.1.X         | 19.07.1 and later, master                                        |
| 1.2.0, 1.2.1  | 19.07.2 and master fails "invalid type"                          |
| 1.3.3         | 23.05.3 flashes successfully, 24.10.0-rc6 flashes successfully   |
| 1.3.4         | 19.07.4 flashes successfully                                     |
| 1.3.5         | 19.07.5 flashes successfully                                     |
| 1.3.6         | 19.07.4 flashes successfully, 21.02, 23.05 flashes successfully  |

If the above images get rejected and you still want to flash through the web interface, start with the current stable release and work your way down through versions. Eventually, you may come across something that gets accepted, allowing you to upgrade to a stable OpenWrt build from that point.

This is going to be a pain, expect a patch to generate factory images for several OEM firmware versions eventually.

It is possible to bypass this check by flashing via the bootloader's TFTP recovery mode, however several steps and a lot of tries are necessary to get everything right, as the link training window is extremely tight. For more information, see the Flash using TFTP recovery section below.
</WRAP>

If your OEM firmware supports it, simply upload openwrt-ath79-generic-tplink_archer-c6-v2-squashfs-factory.bin via the router's web interface. The same image also applies to the Archer A6 v2.

==== Flash using TFTP recovery ====

<WRAP dangerous>
An ethernet switch between the router and the TFTP server should be used in order to prevent the TFTP server's interface to shutdown!\\
If the server's interface shuts down, the router times out and it won't find the TFTP server (see unsuccessful log output below).
</WRAP>

  - Connect to your router's WAN port (it doesn't work with LAN ports!) to a network switch or a computer
  - Connect another cable from the switch to the computer/TFTP server (or directly)
  - Set the computer IP to 192.168.0.66
  - Start a tftp server with the OpenWrt factory image in the tftp root directory renamed to ArcherC6v2_tp_recovery.bin
  - Connect the power cable to the router, press and hold the reset button and turn the router on
  - Keep the reset button pressed until the WPS LED lights up
  - Wait ~150 seconds until flashing and booting is completed

If you're having TFTP time out, it is caused by taking too long to negotiate the link. Use 10/100MBit switch. see Post [[https://forum.openwrt.org/t/cant-boot-tftp-recovery-archer-c6-v2/36487/26]].


